import{_ as e,o as r,c as a,ag as o}from"./chunks/framework.2cFhd8Ux.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"The Empirical Study","description":"","frontmatter":{"source":"graduate/empirical-study.md"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"graduate/empirical-study.md","filePath":"graduate/empirical-study.md"}'),s={name:"graduate/empirical-study.md"};function n(i,t,d,l,h,u){return r(),a("div",null,[...t[0]||(t[0]=[o('<h1 id="the-empirical-study" tabindex="-1">The Empirical Study <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-empirical-study" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The Empirical Study&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>A small, real piece of research: replicate or extend a published result about AI in software engineering, at $0, with open-weight models and public data or your own artifacts, and report it in a short paper. It is the graduate version of the course&#39;s habit of checking claims instead of trusting them.</p></blockquote><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Details</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Who</strong></td><td>CS [5xxx] students, alone or in a pair</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Scope</strong></td><td>A replication or extension from the <a href="#choosing-a-study">replicable list</a>, or a proposal the instructor approves</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cost</strong></td><td>$0: the lab machine&#39;s local models, free tiers under the data rule, public datasets</td></tr><tr><td><strong>People</strong></td><td><strong>No human-subjects research</strong> without IRB approval (<a href="#no-human-subjects-research">rules</a>)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Milestones</strong></td><td>Protocol (week 6) · interim results (week 11) · paper draft (week 14) · 10-minute talk (week 15) · 4–6 page paper in ACM format (finals week)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Graded as</strong></td><td>11% of the course: protocol 1.5% · interim results 1.5% · talk 2% · paper 6% (<a href="#grading">grading</a>)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>AI use</strong></td><td>🟡 AI limited: analysis code may be AI-assisted and is disclosed; the research question, the design and the prose are yours (<a href="#ai-use">details</a>)</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="choosing-a-study" tabindex="-1">Choosing a study <a class="header-anchor" href="#choosing-a-study" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Choosing a study&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="the-replicable-list" tabindex="-1">The replicable list <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-replicable-list" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The replicable list&quot;">​</a></h3><p>These five studies from the course&#39;s evidence review can each be replicated or extended in about four weeks of part-time work, at $0, with open-weight models and public data (<a href="./../research/notes/evidence-senior.html">evidence notes</a>; also on the reading list as the <a href="./../resources/reading-list.html#replicable-studies">replicable studies</a>). The one-line plan is a starting point; your protocol makes it precise.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Study</th><th>What it found</th><th>A plan at $0</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Liang, Garg and Zilouchian Moghaddam</strong>, &quot;The SWE-Bench Illusion&quot; (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.12286" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">arXiv 2506.12286</a>)</td><td>From issue text alone, models named the buggy file far more often on SWE-bench than on other repositories, a sign of memorization</td><td>Run the file-path probe with 2–3 open-weight models on the lab machine, on 200 SWE-bench Verified issues and 200 issues created after the models&#39; training cutoffs (from SWE-rebench or SWE-bench-Live), and compare the two rates with bootstrap confidence intervals</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Zhao, Zhou and Cohen</strong>, coverage and mutation scores of LLM-generated suites (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.22880" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">arXiv 2607.22880</a>)</td><td>Coverage and mutation scores of LLM-written suites track real-bug detection in regression settings, but not when the code may already be buggy</td><td>Generate pytest suites with one local model for 10 BugsInPy projects (buggy and fixed versions), measure coverage.py and mutmut scores, and correlate both with whether each suite detects the real bug</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Chowdhury et al.</strong>, code-review agents in pull requests (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.03196" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">arXiv 2604.03196</a>)</td><td>PRs reviewed only by review agents merged less often than human-reviewed ones, and most agents&#39; comments had low signal</td><td>Repeat the merge-rate and signal analysis on a newer slice of the AIDev dataset or GH Archive, and hand-code 100 review comments with two raters, reporting Cohen&#39;s κ. Best as a pair study: you and your partner are the raters. Work with the data in aggregate and name no individuals</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Tam et al.</strong> and <strong>.txt&#39;s</strong> rebuttal, structured output and reasoning (<a href="https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-industry.91/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">EMNLP 2024</a>; <a href="https://blog.dottxt.ai/say-what-you-mean.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Say What You Mean</a>)</td><td>Tam et al. found format restrictions hurt reasoning; .txt traced that to mismatched prompts and an LLM answer parser</td><td>With two local 7–8B models and Outlines or llama.cpp grammars, cross prompt (matched or mismatched) × decoding (free or constrained) × parser (regex or LLM) on GSM8K and Last Letter</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Thakur et al.</strong>, &quot;Judging the Judges&quot; (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.12624" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">arXiv 2406.12624</a>)</td><td>Only the largest judges approached human alignment, and high percent agreement hid large score gaps</td><td>Rerun the comparison on the paper&#39;s released labels with 3–4 small open-weight judges, add one new bias test (answer order or verbosity), and report κ alongside percent agreement</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="your-own-proposal" tabindex="-1">Your own proposal <a class="header-anchor" href="#your-own-proposal" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Your own proposal&quot;">​</a></h3><p>You may propose a different study, using <strong>public data or your own artifacts</strong>: code you wrote, agent runs you performed, labels you made yourself. Propose it to the instructor by the end of week 5. Ideas that build on the course&#39;s labs:</p><ul><li><strong>Agent-written tests versus yours.</strong> Do tests an agent writes kill fewer mutants than tests you write for the same <code>roomly</code> modules, across more modules and a second model? No course has published results on mutation testing of AI-written tests, so this is an open question (<a href="./../research/notes/courses-upper-division.html">course notes</a>).</li><li><strong>Does an <code>AGENTS.md</code> change agent behavior?</strong> Extend your week 3 A/B test to 20 or more tasks on the class codebase, with a criterion fixed in advance. An observational study found less complexity growth in repositories with a committed configuration file (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.25241" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Denisov-Blanch et al.</a>); yours would be an experiment on your own runs.</li><li><strong>How stable is an LLM judge?</strong> Your week 12 eval set, several judges, answer-order and prompt variations, with κ against your own labels.</li></ul><p>A good proposal has a question with an answer you don&#39;t already know, a design that could prove you wrong, a measure that fits the question, and runs that fit the lab machine&#39;s queue.</p><h2 id="no-human-subjects-research" tabindex="-1">No human-subjects research <a class="header-anchor" href="#no-human-subjects-research" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;No human-subjects research&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Without approval from the University&#39;s Institutional Review Board (IRB), your study involves no people.</strong> In practice, that means:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Not allowed without IRB approval</th><th>Allowed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Surveys, interviews or experiments with anyone, including classmates</td><td>Public datasets, used in aggregate and under their licenses</td></tr><tr><td>Studying classmates&#39; course work: their PRs, reviews, <code>AI_LOG.md</code> entries, peer evaluations or grades, even where the repository is public</td><td>Your own artifacts: your code, your agent runs, your labels</td></tr><tr><td>Studying maintainers: submitting PRs, issues or comments as an experiment, or measuring how people respond to you</td><td>Model outputs you generate</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The cautionary case is real: in 2021 the Linux kernel banned contributions from the University of Minnesota after a research group submitted deliberately flawed patches to study the review process (<a href="https://www.theregister.com/2021/04/21/minnesota_linux_kernel_flaws_update/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">The Register</a>; <a href="https://lwn.net/Articles/854076/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">LWN</a>). If your idea involves people in any way, talk to the instructor <strong>before</strong> writing the protocol. IRB review takes weeks, so in a one-term study the answer is almost always to change the design. If you hope to publish your study later, say so early.</p><h2 id="compute-and-data-at-0" tabindex="-1">Compute and data at $0 <a class="header-anchor" href="#compute-and-data-at-0" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Compute and data at $0&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>The lab machine</strong> serves open-weight models through Ollama to the whole class, as one queue. Plan batch runs early, run them overnight or at quiet times, and log every run.</li><li><strong>Free API tiers</strong> are fine for public data only, under the course&#39;s data rule: free tiers may train on your prompts.</li><li><strong>Budget your runs</strong> in your protocol: number of items × conditions × repetitions × models, and what you&#39;ll cut if it doesn&#39;t fit.</li><li><strong>Spending caps at zero</strong> on anything metered, as the <a href="./../setup/safety-contract.html">agent safety contract</a> says.</li><li><strong>Name exact versions.</strong> The course&#39;s materials keep model names and versions in TOOLS.md, but your paper is research: record the exact model, version or tag, quantization, context length, seed and prompt for every run, or nobody can reproduce it.</li></ul><h2 id="milestones" tabindex="-1">Milestones <a class="header-anchor" href="#milestones" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Milestones&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Everything is submitted on Canvas. Code and data that you&#39;re allowed to share go in a public repository (your artifact), linked from each submission.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Milestone</th><th>Due</th><th>What you submit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Protocol</strong></td><td>Before the week 7 Seminar</td><td>The protocol, from <a href="./../templates/STUDY_PROTOCOL.html">STUDY_PROTOCOL.md template</a>: research question and hypotheses; what you replicate and what you change; design, materials and measures; the analysis you&#39;ll run, decided now; the run budget; threats to validity you can foresee; a timeline; and a statement that no people are involved</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Interim results</strong></td><td>Before the week 12 Seminar</td><td>Two pages: the pipeline running end to end, results for at least one condition with their uncertainty, what went differently from the protocol, and the revised plan</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Paper draft</strong></td><td>Before the week 15 Seminar</td><td>The complete paper as far as it can be written; feedback comes back within a week</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Talk</strong></td><td>The week 15 Seminar</td><td>10 minutes plus 2–3 minutes of questions. With more than about four graduate students, talks continue into the Lab</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Final paper</strong></td><td>11:59 pm on the last day of finals week</td><td>4–6 pages in ACM format, plus the link to your artifact</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="the-paper" tabindex="-1">The paper <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-paper" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The paper&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Format:</strong> ACM&#39;s two-column conference format (the <code>acmart</code> LaTeX class with the <code>sigconf</code> option, or ACM&#39;s Word template), <strong>4–6 pages</strong> excluding references.</li><li><strong>Sections:</strong> introduction (question and why it matters); the original study and what you replicate or extend; method; results; threats to validity; related work; conclusion; an <strong>artifact</strong> statement linking your code, data (where the license allows), seeds and exact commands.</li><li><strong>Report what the protocol said you would</strong>, and every deviation from it with its reason. A replication that fails to reproduce the original result is a perfectly good result, if it&#39;s done carefully.</li></ul><h2 id="grading" tabindex="-1">Grading <a class="header-anchor" href="#grading" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Grading&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="protocol-10-points-15" tabindex="-1">Protocol (10 points, 1.5%) <a class="header-anchor" href="#protocol-10-points-15" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Protocol (10 points, 1.5%)&quot;">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Points</th><th>What earns them</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Question and hypotheses</td><td>3</td><td>A question you can answer by the end of term, and a hypothesis your design could refute</td></tr><tr><td>Design and measures</td><td>3</td><td>Conditions, materials and measures that fit the question; what is replicated and what changes</td></tr><tr><td>Analysis plan</td><td>2</td><td>The statistics decided in advance (for example a bootstrap interval, a paired comparison, κ)</td></tr><tr><td>Feasibility</td><td>2</td><td>A run budget that fits the lab machine and $0, a timeline, and the no-human-subjects statement</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="interim-results-10-points-15" tabindex="-1">Interim results (10 points, 1.5%) <a class="header-anchor" href="#interim-results-10-points-15" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Interim results (10 points, 1.5%)&quot;">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Points</th><th>What earns them</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pipeline</td><td>4</td><td>Runs end to end on real data, logged and repeatable</td></tr><tr><td>First results</td><td>3</td><td>Numbers for at least one condition, with their uncertainty, interpreted honestly</td></tr><tr><td>Revised plan</td><td>3</td><td>What changed from the protocol, why, and what you&#39;ll do in the remaining weeks</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="talk-20-points-2" tabindex="-1">Talk (20 points, 2%) <a class="header-anchor" href="#talk-20-points-2" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Talk (20 points, 2%)&quot;">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Points</th><th>What earns them</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Question and motivation</td><td>4</td><td>Why the question matters to people building software with AI</td></tr><tr><td>Method</td><td>4</td><td>Clear enough that the audience could judge the design</td></tr><tr><td>Results</td><td>6</td><td>The main numbers with their uncertainty, in readable figures</td></tr><tr><td>Limitations</td><td>3</td><td>The threats to validity, stated before anyone asks</td></tr><tr><td>Questions</td><td>3</td><td>Answers that engage with the question, including &quot;I don&#39;t know, and here&#39;s how I&#39;d find out&quot;</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="paper-100-points-6" tabindex="-1">Paper (100 points, 6%) <a class="header-anchor" href="#paper-100-points-6" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Paper (100 points, 6%)&quot;">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Exemplary</th><th>Proficient</th><th>Developing</th><th>Missing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Question and motivation</strong></td><td><strong>10</strong> · A precise question, why it matters, and what the answer would change</td><td><strong>8</strong> · Clear question and motivation</td><td><strong>4</strong> · Vague question</td><td><strong>0</strong> · Missing</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Relation to the original</strong></td><td><strong>10</strong> · Exactly what is replicated, what is changed, and why; the original&#39;s result stated accurately</td><td><strong>8</strong> · Clear, with a small gap</td><td><strong>4</strong> · Unclear what is replicated</td><td><strong>0</strong> · Missing</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Method</strong></td><td><strong>20</strong> · Follows the protocol, with every deviation and its reason; sample sizes justified; exact models and settings</td><td><strong>15</strong> · Follows the protocol; deviations mostly reported</td><td><strong>8</strong> · Unreported deviations, or a design that can&#39;t answer the question</td><td><strong>0</strong> · Missing</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Results with uncertainty</strong></td><td><strong>20</strong> · Confidence intervals or equivalent; paired comparisons where they apply; κ where there are raters; figures that don&#39;t mislead</td><td><strong>15</strong> · Uncertainty reported for the main results</td><td><strong>8</strong> · Point estimates only</td><td><strong>0</strong> · No results</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Threats to validity</strong></td><td><strong>15</strong> · Construct, internal and external threats specific to this study (contamination, model versions, prompt sensitivity, sample)</td><td><strong>11</strong> · Several specific threats</td><td><strong>5</strong> · Generic threats</td><td><strong>0</strong> · Missing</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reproducibility</strong></td><td><strong>15</strong> · A public artifact with code, seeds, exact model versions and commands, rerunnable at $0; data included or linked where the license allows</td><td><strong>11</strong> · An artifact that reruns with minor effort</td><td><strong>5</strong> · Code without the means to rerun it</td><td><strong>0</strong> · No artifact</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Draft and writing</strong></td><td><strong>10</strong> · A complete draft on time, revised in response to feedback; clear writing in ACM format, within the page limit</td><td><strong>8</strong> · Draft on time; clear writing</td><td><strong>4</strong> · Late or partial draft, or hard to follow</td><td><strong>0</strong> · No draft and unclear writing</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="working-in-a-pair" tabindex="-1">Working in a pair <a class="header-anchor" href="#working-in-a-pair" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Working in a pair&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>A bigger scope:</strong> more models, more conditions, both halves of an extension, or (for a study like Chowdhury et al.&#39;s) the two independent raters that κ needs.</li><li><strong>Both of you write, both of you present.</strong> In the talk, each partner presents part and answers questions.</li><li><strong>A contribution statement</strong> on Canvas (🔴 AI off), agreed by both, says who did what. It stays on Canvas.</li><li><strong>Normally the same score;</strong> if the record shows a large imbalance, the instructor talks with both partners and may adjust individual scores.</li></ul><h2 id="ai-use" tabindex="-1">AI use <a class="header-anchor" href="#ai-use" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;AI use&quot;">​</a></h2><p>🟡 <strong>AI limited.</strong> Analysis code, data-processing scripts and plotting code may be written with AI help; disclose it in the paper&#39;s artifact statement and in your artifact&#39;s <code>AI_LOG.md</code>, with <code>Assisted-by:</code> trailers on its commits. The research question, the design, and the prose of the protocol, interim report and paper are yours (🔴 AI off). An AI may not generate, label or &quot;clean&quot; your data in any way you don&#39;t report as part of the method; if a model labels data, that&#39;s a condition of your study, not a shortcut. The complete course list is in the <a href="./../assessment/#ai-use-policy">AI use policy</a>.</p>',35)])])}const p=e(s,[["render",n]]);export{g as __pageData,p as default};
